<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tientsin (1885)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> of 9 June 1885 was signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and the French envoy Jules Patenôtre, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> recognised the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> over Annam and Tonkin, giving up its claim to suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a>. It opened the way to the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tientsin-1885.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tientsin (1885)" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Hongzhang_and_Jules_Patenotre_after_signing_the_Li-Patenotre_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In December 1884, alarmed by Japanese ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> ordered her ministers to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> out of the undeclared war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that had broken out on 23 August 1884. French victories in Tonkin and on Formosa in February and early March 1885 strengthened her resolve. A Chinese victory in late March, when General de Négrier's 2nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> was defeated at Bang Bo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> was reoccupied, was offset by the French capture of the Pescadores Islands.</p><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry</a>'s ministry at the end of March gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> an opening. It agreed to carry out the Tianjin Accord of 11 May 1884, and the French dropped their demand for an indemnity for the Bắc Lệ ambush. Preliminaries of peace signed on 4 April 1885 brought a ceasefire in Tonkin and Formosa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> armies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a>'s Black Flag Army withdrew from Tonkin, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> as a surety.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty of ten articles restated the accord of 1884 in greater detail. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> undertook to keep order in the provinces of Annam bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and to respect the frontier, and China pledged not to send troops into Tonkin and to respect the treaties France had made or would make with Annam. Commissioners were to mark the border after six months, and two trading posts were to be opened on the Chinese side, above Lào Cai and above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, with the same advantages for French merchants as in the treaty ports.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was to build roads in Tonkin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, should it build railways, was to turn to French industry, though without granting France an exclusive privilege. French forces were to leave Formosa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> within a month. Hardliners at the Qing court, who blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> for earlier concessions, had insisted that he negotiate alongside two members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, Xi Zhen and Deng Chengxiu of the Purist party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French lifted their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> on 10 June 1885, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> on 21 June and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> on 22 July, and ratifications were exchanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 28 November 1885. A Sino-French commission marked the border between late 1885 and 1887; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> refused Chinese claims to Đồng Đăng but awarded the Bailong peninsula to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and an agreement confirming the line, which largely forms today's China-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> border, was signed in Beijing on 26 June 1887. Discredited by the defeat, the Purists soon lost influence at court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Tianjin (1885)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Tu Duc’s death in 1883 and the killing of the French officer Henri Rivière in Tonkin, France imposed the Harmand Treaty of 1883 and the Patenôtre Treaty of 1884, making Annam and Tonkin protectorates, and the <b>Treaty of Tientsin</b> of 1885 ended Qing suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
